{
  "dom_id": "question-133286",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "The vertical velocity of the eagle increases at a rate proportional to the square of the time $t.$ In addition, the eagle loses $2\\,\\text{m/s}$ in the positive $y$-direction due to wind resistance. It is known that the eagle's vertical velocity equals $2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The differential equation that describes the eagle's vertical velocity is ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "The vertical velocity increases proportionally with ${t}^{2}$ and decreases by $2.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=k{t}^{2}-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality.\n\nSince ${y}^{′}(2)=2,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(2) & =2 \\\\ k(2)^{2}-2 & =2 \\\\ 4k & =4 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
    {
      "key": "field-1",
